\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces A CAD diagram of the Big Red Ball. The experiment starts with a background field generated by a Helmholtz coil (1) into which plasma is injected via a set of plasma guns (2). After some time, the plasma has equilibrated and then a reverse field is driven via either a set of individual coils or a drive cylinder (3). A reconnecting current layer travels radially inward from high radius to low, passing probes (4) along the way giving us the ability to trace the plasma parameters from the low radius inflow, into a reconnection outflow, and through the high radius inflow.}}{13}{figure.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:brb_cad_rendering}{{1.5}{13}{A CAD diagram of the Big Red Ball. The experiment starts with a background field generated by a Helmholtz coil (1) into which plasma is injected via a set of plasma guns (2). After some time, the plasma has equilibrated and then a reverse field is driven via either a set of individual coils or a drive cylinder (3). A reconnecting current layer travels radially inward from high radius to low, passing probes (4) along the way giving us the ability to trace the plasma parameters from the low radius inflow, into a reconnection outflow, and through the high radius inflow}{figure.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{Diagnostics Overview}{13}{section*.34}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sss:diagnostics_overview}{{1.3}{13}{Diagnostics Overview}{section*.34}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces Timeline of a single shot for TREX. The permanent magnets always generate a 0.4 T magnetic field at their surfaces that decays quickly into a minimal background field in the bulk of the machine. About four seconds before the start of the shot, the Helmholtz coil is turned on to allow the field to penetrate the three inch thick aluminum wall. Plasma is injected at 12 ms along with an optional toroidal field coil. The drive coils are then pulsed with a ringing current. The first quarter period, which will be analyzed throughout this thesis, is where a pressure wave travels radially inwards, followed by a shock, and finally a reconnection layer.}}{14}{figure.caption.32}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:experimental_sequence_timeline}{{1.6}{14}{Timeline of a single shot for TREX. The permanent magnets always generate a 0.4 T magnetic field at their surfaces that decays quickly into a minimal background field in the bulk of the machine. About four seconds before the start of the shot, the Helmholtz coil is turned on to allow the field to penetrate the three inch thick aluminum wall. Plasma is injected at 12 ms along with an optional toroidal field coil. The drive coils are then pulsed with a ringing current. The first quarter period, which will be analyzed throughout this thesis, is where a pressure wave travels radially inwards, followed by a shock, and finally a reconnection layer}{figure.caption.32}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.7}{\ignorespaces Measurements from two flux arrays for a single shot. Each flux array measures $\partial _t B_z$ at many $r$ and $z$ locations in a single $\phi $ plane. Each column is a different time in the experiment and each row shows a different parameter. The black line is the location where $B_z = 0$ and the grey lines are example magnetic fields. The top left plot shows the reconnection layer around $B_z = 0$ with $\partial _t B_z > 0$ at low radii caused by a shock. The top middle and right plots show the layer moving inwards. $B_z$ can be seen to change signs across the x-point, starting at a positive field set by the Helmholtz coil and ending with the reversed field from the drive coils. The bottom three plots show the out of plane current density which is peaked at $B_z = 0$ which is the reconnection layer.}}{15}{figure.caption.33}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flux_array_movie_frames}{{1.7}{15}{Measurements from two flux arrays for a single shot. Each flux array measures $\partial _t B_z$ at many $r$ and $z$ locations in a single $\phi $ plane. Each column is a different time in the experiment and each row shows a different parameter. The black line is the location where $B_z = 0$ and the grey lines are example magnetic fields. The top left plot shows the reconnection layer around $B_z = 0$ with $\partial _t B_z > 0$ at low radii caused by a shock. The top middle and right plots show the layer moving inwards. $B_z$ can be seen to change signs across the x-point, starting at a positive field set by the Helmholtz coil and ending with the reversed field from the drive coils. The bottom three plots show the out of plane current density which is peaked at $B_z = 0$ which is the reconnection layer}{figure.caption.33}{}}
